      "text": "**Solution 1**: We notice that 11 is between 9 and 1*10. Thus, the target digit members will start from 110 and call segment with length 9, making 2 members. The next 9 numbers will have a 2, which means we need the segment with decent number of member (3) ending in 1. Starting from 2128, 3591, and ending at 6574, 8783 make the club. So, we finish here and send the segment with 7 member. To wrap up, we will have 11 times 7 plus one more number that is 1 more than the one that will come next, so 77+1=78, an answer is $\\boxed{78}$.\n\n**Solution 2**: We notice that 11 is between 9 and 1*10, meaning the third term in our number being 110, so it's\n$$5+50+110+165+230+296+363+\\ldots+777$$\nand the 11 terms is very obvious.\n\n**Solution 3**: We notice that as 29, 38, 47 are the first three numbers, the common difference d=28, and the first term a=$29-3*28=1$.\nTherefore, $$T_{11}=1+10d=111$$.\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{111}$.\n\n*shorter solutions are also appreciated.*\n\nWe have that 11 is between 9 and 1*1000. We have 100 possibilities for the first group of numbers. So, the first group of numbers is from the 3rd group's 9th term because 9 is between 9 and 1*100. So we have the whole third group for the first group of numbers, 1 set of the second group, 2 sets of the first group, and 1 set of the first group. Since the term number and the group number goes up by one every time, we have that $T_{11}=100+9+8+5+1+5=111$, which is our answer $\\boxed{111}$.\n\n* Cool Check: When we put 11 into 29+11, we get 40, when we put 2 into 40+11 we get 51, and so on. Each term we add 11 increments the number. $111 is between 110 and 2*111-1.$ When we split our 11 into sets of 9 we have 3 terms. That means we only have to check 9 from 110-3*11 because the interval 9 between the groups of numbers will be a multiple of the common difference of 11. So, by checking 111 we see that that is the answer $\\Box$",
