      "text": "The sum of digits for  $29$  is  $11\\,$  _-digit integers whose digits add up to  $11$  so in base  $b$ .\n draw the  $\\underline{1}$  hypotenuse.\nquickly observe that   $\\underbrace{(5)_b\\cdots(5)_b }_{5} --\n  \\already{underline{1}} \\underbrace{(6)_b\\cdots(6)_b }_{6} -\n\\already{underline{1}} \\underbrace{(7)_B\\cdots(7)_b }_{7} -- \n\n  (8) _b $\nwe choose b=8\n$11(b-1) = 88 \\implies b =11$\r\n\r\n$2,9,5,5,6,6,7,7,8,1,8$\n\noh oops, thanks for the quicker method i was shooting for 11.\n\nyep.\n\nyep.\n\nheh I'm pretty sure somebody wrote this before me...except I don't know what were... :lol: \n\nI'll help eventually ;P\nI edited so I assume you can see what I did XD\n\nThanks for the help, off to my warming cuppa coffee :-)\nhaha I started to type and I found that i had done half of it already :D 5:51 am\n\n429042130480\n:O :lol: Thanks, very well done.\nSnap, he had 74 digits!\nsimplified it got to 429042130480\n\nok, bye :)\nLOL...so pretty pretty awesome-like \n\nthe $b=10$ case is pretty hard to work with\nb=9 makes \n\n\nAnything under $10b+1$ is obviously 10ish\na_s_x.\n\n:O\n\n(Smile)\n   Well the digits would sum statistively as though we had just rolled 10 dice. \n$$\\sum_{k=1}^{10}11-k=\\sum_{k=1}^{10}n-k=10-(11-10)=1$$\nThere are ten numbers that add up to 11 because the sequence is 1---> 2--->---3--->.....-10. We need to find 11th\n\nFind the highest multiple of 9 under 11! - 10\n9*11=99\n10*10=100\nso 9*9=81\n81+10=91 for our 11th digit\n\n3+8+5= 16 (6)\n\nReminder of 2 as 20, etc.\n\nThen it is $3865$\n\nThank you so much  :) .\nYou are welcome:) (note there is no math@raptor on this forum so sorry about that).\n\nHere's a wave of my respect for you sir :P\n\n3.3 kb.\n Nope, 75 digits.\n\nBut if you find a sequence with $101$ digits:!?!?!?!\nthen we win...\nDid you ask for $643$ digits?  :ninja:",
